The problem here is that highlight.js’s built-in “TitleCase” matcher (used by the JS grammar and thus pulled into TS) only looks for an uppercase letter followed by one or more *lowercase* letters.  As soon as it sees another capital (“T” in “OutT”) it stops, so only “Out” gets the title-case styling and the trailing “T” is left un-highlighted.

In order to fully catch mixed-case identifiers like `OutT`, we need to override that default title-case mode in our TypeScript language definition so its regex becomes something like `/[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_]*/` (i.e. uppercase + any number of letters/numbers/underscores), which will consume the entire `OutT`.

Below is a single *SEARCH/REPLACE* patch to insert just before the final `return tsLanguage;` in `src/languages/typescript.js`.  It finds the existing title mode and replaces it with the expanded regex.

```javascript
### src/languages/typescript.js
<<<<<<< SEARCH
  Object.assign(tsLanguage, {
    name: 'TypeScript',
    aliases: ['ts', 'tsx']
  });

  return tsLanguage;
=======
  Object.assign(tsLanguage, {
    name: 'TypeScript',
    aliases: ['ts', 'tsx']
  });

  // Override the default “title” mode so PascalCase identifiers
  // like OutT (not just Out) are fully matched.
  {
    const titleIndex = tsLanguage.contains.findIndex(m => m.className === 'title');
    if (titleIndex !== -1) {
      tsLanguage.contains[titleIndex] = {
        className: 'title',
        begin: /[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_]*/,
        relevance: 0
      };
    }
  }

  return tsLanguage;
>>>>>>> REPLACE
```

With this in place, `OutT`, `InT`, or any other mixed-case type parameter will be entirely highlighted as a single “title” token.
